1
00:00:00,656 --> 00:00:03,637
Now, again, back at this project I showed

2
00:00:03,637 --> 00:00:06,152
you before, this research project here,

3
00:00:06,841 --> 00:00:09,292
there is something else that's interesting

4
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:11,696
in here. Because in this project, we can

5
00:00:11,792 --> 00:00:15,829
actually see 2 types of memory being used.

6
00:00:15,958 --> 00:00:18,906
And memory management is an important

7
00:00:19,803 --> 00:00:20,508
concept

8
00:00:21,085 --> 00:00:23,889
when working with AI models and agents. And

9
00:00:23,905 --> 00:00:26,196
with that, I don't mean your computer

10
00:00:26,372 --> 00:00:28,150
memory. I don't mean your hardware

11
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:30,846
resources. Instead, I'm talking about the

12
00:00:30,846 --> 00:00:33,496
memory of your agents, so how data is

13
00:00:33,704 --> 00:00:34,331
stored.

14
00:00:35,198 --> 00:00:37,221
And here we can typically differentiate

15
00:00:37,221 --> 00:00:38,393
between short-term

16
00:00:39,051 --> 00:00:40,561
and long-term memory.

17
00:00:41,299 --> 00:00:44,013
And the short-term memory is relevant for

18
00:00:44,029 --> 00:00:44,944
information that

19
00:00:45,570 --> 00:00:49,600
belongs to one session, to one agent, maybe

20
00:00:49,681 --> 00:00:51,415
something like the chat history

21
00:00:52,073 --> 00:00:54,964
or results derived by one agent. That would

22
00:00:54,964 --> 00:00:58,022
be short-term because it's discarded once

23
00:00:58,022 --> 00:01:00,886
the program ends or once an agent is done.

24
00:01:02,569 --> 00:01:05,494
It's therefore often stored in computer

25
00:01:05,494 --> 00:01:07,294
memory, so in your RAM,

26
00:01:07,888 --> 00:01:10,363
or maybe in other short-term data stores.

27
00:01:10,877 --> 00:01:13,386
You could be using a memory-only database or

28
00:01:13,466 --> 00:01:14,222
something like this.

29
00:01:14,881 --> 00:01:16,810
You could be using something like Redis if

30
00:01:16,810 --> 00:01:18,482
you're building a more complex system.

31
00:01:19,237 --> 00:01:21,841
It's short-term though. So if you lose the

32
00:01:21,841 --> 00:01:24,087
data, that's no problem and that's indeed

33
00:01:24,168 --> 00:01:26,943
intended. The idea is not to keep this

34
00:01:26,943 --> 00:01:28,804
information, this data around forever.

35
00:01:29,671 --> 00:01:31,901
Now it can be shared across multiple agents

36
00:01:32,173 --> 00:01:34,628
either by returning data and passing it as

37
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:37,291
an input to the next agent as I did it in my

38
00:01:37,291 --> 00:01:37,805
example

39
00:01:38,334 --> 00:01:40,757
or by storing it in some global variable or

40
00:01:40,901 --> 00:01:41,623
anything like that.

41
00:01:42,602 --> 00:01:44,976
And then you also have long-term memory and

42
00:01:45,409 --> 00:01:47,736
that's about storing information across

43
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:50,783
multiple sessions or executions. So data

44
00:01:50,863 --> 00:01:52,915
that should not be lost when the program

45
00:01:53,091 --> 00:01:54,598
ends or an agent is done.

46
00:01:55,752 --> 00:01:58,061
Here we're talking about things like user

47
00:01:58,061 --> 00:02:00,946
preferences or maybe results you wanna use

48
00:02:00,946 --> 00:02:01,652
in the future.

49
00:02:02,341 --> 00:02:04,809
Since it should be stored long-term, this

50
00:02:04,809 --> 00:02:06,380
kind of data is typically stored in

51
00:02:06,477 --> 00:02:07,823
databases or files,

52
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:11,109
either on your computer or on some server in

53
00:02:11,141 --> 00:02:12,151
the cloud, wherever.

54
00:02:12,889 --> 00:02:14,764
And of course, it may also be accessed by

55
00:02:14,764 --> 00:02:16,159
multiple agents because

56
00:02:16,688 --> 00:02:18,916
it's just in a database. Any agent that

57
00:02:18,916 --> 00:02:21,128
needs access to it can reach out to it if

58
00:02:21,304 --> 00:02:23,565
you provide the right logic or the right

59
00:02:23,565 --> 00:02:24,367
tools, of course.

60
00:02:25,089 --> 00:02:26,885
And in this demo application here, we

61
00:02:26,997 --> 00:02:27,783
actually have both.

62
00:02:28,617 --> 00:02:32,481
This research planner agent, for example,

63
00:02:32,930 --> 00:02:35,961
manages a chat history. It stores all the

64
00:02:35,977 --> 00:02:38,062
user messages in the chat history, and it

65
00:02:38,062 --> 00:02:40,852
stores all its own replies in that history.

66
00:02:41,750 --> 00:02:44,188
And that's important because whenever you're

67
00:02:44,268 --> 00:02:46,048
sending a prompt to

68
00:02:46,657 --> 00:02:49,079
a model, to an AI model, no matter if it's a

69
00:02:49,079 --> 00:02:51,661
model by OpenAI, if it's an open model

70
00:02:51,773 --> 00:02:53,859
hosted on your machine, Doesn't matter.

71
00:02:54,116 --> 00:02:55,177
Whenever you do that,

72
00:02:55,707 --> 00:02:58,792
this model only sees what you send it. So if

73
00:02:58,792 --> 00:03:01,396
you don't include an entire chat history,

74
00:03:02,054 --> 00:03:04,047
then this history is not available to it.

75
00:03:04,722 --> 00:03:06,698
Now, for some prompts, for many use cases,

76
00:03:06,795 --> 00:03:09,028
this might not matter. You might only need

77
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:11,824
one prompt. But here, for example, where the

78
00:03:11,856 --> 00:03:13,945
entire history, the entire discussion,

79
00:03:14,106 --> 00:03:16,741
including results of tool calls and so on,

80
00:03:17,079 --> 00:03:19,840
where this all matters, It's crucial that

81
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:22,437
you manage this short-term memory, that you

82
00:03:22,453 --> 00:03:25,322
manage this chat history so that you can

83
00:03:25,418 --> 00:03:28,111
send it to the AI model with future requests

84
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:31,029
so that this history is available to the

85
00:03:31,029 --> 00:03:32,888
model. That's the idea here.

86
00:03:33,770 --> 00:03:36,223
It's then discarded when the agent is done

87
00:03:36,335 --> 00:03:38,242
because we're not doing anything with that

88
00:03:38,242 --> 00:03:39,541
chat history thereafter.

89
00:03:40,743 --> 00:03:43,436
We are, however, of course, returning

90
00:03:44,013 --> 00:03:46,642
our research plan and you could treat that

91
00:03:46,818 --> 00:03:49,784
as short-term memory as well because that

92
00:03:49,944 --> 00:03:52,253
research plan is then handed off to the next

93
00:03:52,349 --> 00:03:53,054
agent in line.

94
00:03:53,968 --> 00:03:56,773
So that is something to keep in mind. But

95
00:03:56,773 --> 00:03:58,793
that's all short-term memory.

96
00:03:59,643 --> 00:04:02,031
But this demo application here also has

97
00:04:02,208 --> 00:04:05,238
long-term memory because we have a database.

98
00:04:05,590 --> 00:04:08,171
And in that database, I'm storing research

99
00:04:08,299 --> 00:04:08,684
plans.

100
00:04:09,902 --> 00:04:12,740
For that, I created some tools that interact

101
00:04:12,788 --> 00:04:15,096
with that database and I'm exposing these

102
00:04:15,209 --> 00:04:16,555
tools to one of my agents.

103
00:04:17,598 --> 00:04:20,085
Therefore, this agent, the research planner

104
00:04:20,085 --> 00:04:22,236
agent, which has access to these tools, has

105
00:04:22,332 --> 00:04:24,001
access to that long-term storage.

106
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:27,772
And this is a long-term data store because

107
00:04:27,804 --> 00:04:29,842
these research plans, in this case here,

108
00:04:30,275 --> 00:04:31,992
should be available in the future too.

109
00:04:33,035 --> 00:04:35,506
If I start a new research session in the

110
00:04:35,506 --> 00:04:38,122
future, I want to be able to tap into those

111
00:04:38,218 --> 00:04:40,785
plans and reuse them. That's the idea.

112
00:04:41,748 --> 00:04:43,979
And therefore, when you're building your own

113
00:04:44,396 --> 00:04:47,828
agentic systems or AI-powered workflows,

114
00:04:48,582 --> 00:04:50,923
you also, of course, might wanna consider

115
00:04:51,052 --> 00:04:53,473
which kind of data should be stored in the

116
00:04:53,473 --> 00:04:56,280
short term or which kind of data you might

117
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:58,397
wanna persist a bit longer so that you can

118
00:04:58,509 --> 00:04:59,744
also use it in the future.

119
00:05:00,514 --> 00:05:02,663
You don't need to overthink it. Normally,

120
00:05:02,856 --> 00:05:05,101
these decisions will come naturally because

121
00:05:05,101 --> 00:05:07,475
you might need something like a chat history

122
00:05:07,507 --> 00:05:09,399
for the program to work the way it should

123
00:05:09,431 --> 00:05:11,596
work. But still, it's important to know

124
00:05:11,596 --> 00:05:14,313
about these 2 concepts also because you'll

125
00:05:14,313 --> 00:05:16,631
encounter these terms quite a bit when

126
00:05:16,761 --> 00:05:18,835
working with agents created by others.
